Q: Check if users are a member of an AD group - Powershell A: <p>I would do it this way. You only need to run get-adgroup once, so less hits for the AD server. -notmatch will return all nonmatches, so will always be nonempty or true. So I used -notin instead, which is more exact as well.</p>
<pre><code>$userlist = get-content users.txt

$group = 'group_xy'
$groupmembers = Get-ADgroup $group | Get-ADGroupMember 

$result = foreach ($user in $userlist) {
  if ($user -notin $groupmembers.samaccountname) {
    [PSCustomObject]@{
      Name = $user 
      Group = $group 
      Member = $false
    }
  }  
}

$result # | Export-csv result.csv -NoTypeInformation
</code></pre>
<pre><code>Name   Group    Member
----   -----    ------
js2010 group_xy False
</code></pre>
